  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}=$ \n{{field-4}}${}^{∘}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the function $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is not defined at $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$ \n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$  are $x={0}^{∘},\\,{180}^{∘}.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}.$ The solutions to this equation for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ are $x={60}^{∘},\\,{240}^{∘}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={0}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={60}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={240}^{∘}.\n$$",
